# Where the *ValidationTest suite finds real statements to reconcile against.
#
# Copy this file to `statements.local.properties` (gitignored) and fill in the
# folders on your machine. Every key is optional: an unset one makes its test skip,
# which is what happens on CI and on a fresh clone.
#
# THE KEYS BELOW ARE NOT A SCHEMA. They exist because this repo ships three parsers
# written against three specific statement formats — one bank, one card issuer, one
# property manager — and those are the author's. Yours will be different. Nothing here
# requires you to have a Bayside or Crestline statement, or any statement at all: the whole
# mechanism is opt-in, key by key, and a clone that ignores this file entirely builds
# green with every validation test skipped.
#
# Bringing your own is the expected path. Write a parser, write its validation test,
# pick whatever key name you like — LocalStatements resolves any key you ask it for,
# and none of the names here are special to it. The parsers and tests already in the
# repo are reference material showing the shape: reconcile the parse against whatever
# control totals the statement itself prints, and refuse rather than guess.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: these paths used to be hard-coded in the test sources, which
# published a home directory, an account nickname, and the institutions behind each
# parser to anyone who read the repo. Statement PDFs were never committed; the paths
# to them effectively were.
#
# A SKIP IS NOT A PASS. If you have statements and the test skips, this file is wrong.
# The parsers are only proven against real statements here — see README, "Testing".
#
# HOW EACH KEY BEHAVES
#   unset / blank / commented out  -> the test skips, quietly. Nothing was requested.
#   set and correct                -> the test runs.
#   set and pointing at nothing    -> the test FAILS, naming the key and the bad path.
#
# The third case is not a demand that the data exist forever — it is a demand that this
# file and your disk agree, and you can settle that from either side. If a folder moved,
# fix the path. If it is gone for good and you do not want it checked any more, blank the
# key or comment it out; it drops straight back to the first case and stays quiet. What is
# not available is leaving a wrong path in place and having it ignored, because that is
# indistinguishable from the tests still running.
#
# You can also set any key for one run without a file:
#   ./mvnw test -Dpigpurchases.statements.crestline=D:/statements/crestline
# or from the environment, upper-cased with dots as underscores:
#   PIGPURCHASES_STATEMENTS_CHASE=/mnt/statements/crestline

# --- Bayside deposit accounts -------------------------------------------
# Two keys because one login can hold a consumer ("Northwind Checking") and a business
# ("Business Checking") account, and the parser handles both. Set
# either, both, or neither.
bayside.primary=C:/path/to/statements/bayside/primary/2026
bayside.secondary=C:/path/to/statements/bayside/business/2026

# --- Crestline credit card ----------------------------------------------------------
crestline=C:/path/to/statements/crestline/2026

# --- Ridgeline Properties rent/utility statements ----------------------------
ridgeline=C:/path/to/statements/ridgeline

# One statement whose figures you have checked by eye. The exact count, total and utility
# list are the sharpest assertions in the suite, so they are worth setting — but they
# describe YOUR statement, so they live here rather than in the test.
#
# These four travel as a GROUP: set them all, or leave them all out. A half-configured
# sample is rejected, because naming a statement without saying what it should contain
# reads as configured while asserting almost nothing. Leave all four out and the test
# still runs its structural checks and insists at least one PDF parsed.
# ridgeline.sample.file=statement-2026-07-01.pdf
# ridgeline.sample.count=3
# ridgeline.sample.total=123.45
# ridgeline.sample.utilities=Sewer,Trash,Water
